.TH MUTOS_AOUT.H 5 "2026-08-07" "MUTOS 1700 Toolchain" "MUTOS 1700 Cross-Compiler Toolchain"
.SH NAME
mutos_aout.h \- MUTOS 1700 / Unix V7 a.out object and executable file format
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include "mutos_aout.h"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mutos_aout.h
defines, and provides explicit little-endian (de)serialization helpers
for, the on-disk
.I a.out
object and executable file format used by MUTOS 1700 on the Robotron
A7100/A7150 (an x86-16, 8086/NEC-V30-based machine that is binary- and
architecture-compatible with Unix Version 7). It is consumed by every
component of the MUTOS 1700 cross-compiler toolchain that reads or
writes object, executable, or archive files on the 64-bit Linux host
(currently
.IR mutos_ld (1)).
.PP
The header is derived directly from the original Unix V7
.I a_out.h
(referred to in this project as
.IR v7_a_out.h ),
not from the separate, MUTOS-specific
.I a_out.h
found among the historical sources, which additionally defines an
unrelated
.BR x.out / "struct exec"
format used only for MS-DOS compatibility and has no bearing on the
native MUTOS 1700 target.
.PP
.B "Byte order note (important, and easy to get wrong):"
the MUTOS 1700
.I a.out
exec header and symbol table are
.B entirely
16-bit based; no field in either one is a 32-bit
.B long,
so plain little-endian encoding applies throughout them and PDP-11
middle-endian encoding is never needed there. The legacy V7
.IR ar (1)/ranlib (1)
archive format, by contrast, was carried over into MUTOS
.B unmodified
and still uses genuine 32-bit
.B long
fields in PDP-11 middle-endian byte order (high-order 16-bit word
first, each word itself little-endian) for its
.BR atime ,
.B asize
and
.B cloc
fields; see
.B ARCHIVE FORMAT
below. All multi-byte fields, of either kind, are (de)serialized
explicitly by this header's inline helper functions rather than via
direct struct overlay, so that host struct padding and host
endianness never leak into the on-disk representation.
.SH "EXEC HEADER"
Every object file and executable begins with a fixed 16-byte header,
eight 16-bit little-endian words:
.PP
.nf
.ta 1.4i
typedef struct {
    uint16_t magic;   /* a_magic  */
    uint16_t text;    /* a_text   - text segment size (bytes) */
    uint16_t data;    /* a_data   - initialized data size */
    uint16_t bss;     /* a_bss    - uninitialized data size */
    uint16_t syms;    /* a_syms   - symbol table size (bytes) */
    uint16_t entry;   /* a_entry  - entry point */
    uint16_t unused;  /* a_unused - unused */
    uint16_t flag;    /* a_flag   - 1 = relocation info stripped */
} mutos_hdr_t;
.fi
.PP
.B MUTOS_HDR_SIZE
(16) gives the on-disk size of the header in bytes.
.PP
.B magic
selects both the interpretation of the
.B text
/
.B data
layout and, historically, the memory-management policy the kernel
applies when loading the file (shared/pure text, separate I&D, etc.);
see
.B "MAGIC NUMBERS"
below.
.B text,
.B data
and
.B bss
give the size in bytes of the text, initialized-data, and
uninitialized-data (bss) segments respectively; MUTOS
.IR ld (1)
unconditionally pads the physical end of
.B text
up to an 8-byte boundary (and, for
.B NMAGIC
/
.B IMAGIC
output, further to a 64-byte boundary) before the
.B data
segment begins on disk \(em see
.IR mutos_ld (1)
for the exact padding rule and fill patterns; this differs from stock
V7, where such padding is conditional on those same magic numbers.
.B syms
gives the size in bytes of the trailing symbol table (a multiple of
.BR MUTOS_SYM_SIZE ,
12).
.B entry
is the entry point address; by convention the low bit is set to 1 when
an explicit entry symbol was supplied via
.IR ld (1)'s
.B \-e
option.
.B unused
is reserved and always written as 0.
.B flag
is nonzero if relocation information has been stripped from the file;
.IR mutos_ld (1)
always requires this flag to be clear on its input files and rejects
any file with it set.
.SS "Header layout in the file"
.PP
.nf
.ta 2.6i
offset 0                        header (16 bytes, MUTOS_HDR_SIZE)
offset 16                       text (a_text bytes)
offset 16+text                  data (a_data bytes)
offset 16+text+data             text relocation (a_text bytes)  [if present]
offset 16+2*text+data           data relocation (a_data bytes)  [if present]
offset 16+2*(text+data)         symbol table (a_syms bytes)     [if present]
.fi
.PP
The relocation tables are present unless
.B a_flag
is set (relocation stripped); the symbol table is present unless
.B a_syms
is 0. Both are conventionally omitted together from a final,
non-relocatable executable produced with
.IR ld (1)'s
.B \-s
option, but the two are controlled independently by the header.
.SS "MAGIC NUMBERS"
.TP
.B A_MAGIC1
(0407,
.BR FMAGIC )
Normal format: text and data are contiguous, loaded read-write, and
not shared between processes running the same executable.
.TP
.B A_MAGIC2
(0410,
.BR NMAGIC )
Read-only, shareable text: the
.B data
segment origin is additionally rounded up to a page-style boundary
past the end of
.B text
(see
.IR mutos_ld (1)).
.TP
.B A_MAGIC3
(0411,
.BR IMAGIC )
Separate instruction and data (I&D) address spaces: the
.B data
segment's in-memory address space starts fresh at address 0, distinct
from the
.B text
address space, even though
.B text
and
.B data
remain physically contiguous within the disk file itself.
.TP
.B A_MAGIC4
(0405,
.BR OMAGIC )
Overlay / plain unshared binary; no additional segment-origin rounding
is applied.
.SH "SYMBOL TABLE ENTRY"
Each symbol table entry is a fixed 12 bytes
.RB ( MUTOS_SYM_SIZE ):
.PP
.nf
.ta 1.4i
typedef struct {
    char     name[8];
    uint8_t  type;    /* N_EXT | N_UNDF/N_ABS/N_TEXT/N_DATA/N_BSS */
    uint8_t  spare;   /* filler byte, always written as 0 */
    uint16_t value;
} mutos_sym_t;
.fi
.PP
.B name
is the symbol name, NUL-padded to 8 bytes (not necessarily
NUL-terminated if all 8 bytes are significant).
.B type
encodes both the symbol's segment/kind and whether it is external; see
.B "SYMBOL TYPES"
below.
.B spare
corresponds to the upper byte of the original's 16-bit
.B n_type
field, which MUTOS never uses; it is always written as 0 and should be
ignored (not necessarily 0) on read.
.B value
is the symbol's value: for a defined symbol, its address relative to
the start of its segment (in the input; absolute in final output); for
an external undefined symbol referenced with a nonzero value, its
requested common-block size in bytes (see
.B "COMMON SYMBOLS" ).
.SS "SYMBOL TYPES"
.TP
.B N_TYPE
(0x1F) Mask isolating the segment/kind bits of
.BR type ,
before the
.B N_EXT
bit.
.TP
.B N_UNDF
(0x00) Undefined.
.TP
.B N_ABS
(0x01) Absolute (segment-independent) value.
.TP
.B N_TEXT
(0x02) Defined relative to the text segment.
.TP
.B N_DATA
(0x03) Defined relative to the data segment.
.TP
.B N_BSS
(0x04) Defined relative to the bss segment.
.TP
.B N_COMM
(0x05) Common symbol; used internally by
.IR ld (1)
while resolving tentative (common) definitions and never appears in a
symbol table actually written to disk (see
.B "COMMON SYMBOLS" ).
.TP
.B N_EXT
(0x20) External-linkage bit, OR'ed into any of the above; e.g.
.B "N_EXT|N_TEXT"
is an external symbol defined in text.
.SS "COMMON SYMBOLS"
An external symbol that appears as
.B "N_EXT|N_UNDF"
with a nonzero
.B value
is a tentative (Fortran/K&R-style "common") definition: the value
gives the requested size in bytes rather than an address.
.IR ld (1)
assigns storage for all such symbols out of a synthetic block appended
to
.B bss
once the link is otherwise fully resolved, at which point each
becomes an ordinary
.B "N_EXT|N_BSS"
definition in the output; the type
.B N_COMM
is only ever seen as a transient in-memory state during that
assignment and is not itself a valid on-disk type.
.SH "RELOCATION FORMAT"
Each 16-bit word of the
.B text
or
.B data
segment has a corresponding 16-bit relocation tag at the same
segment-relative offset in the section's relocation table. A tag of 0
means the corresponding content word needs no relocation and is used
as-is.
.PP
A nonzero tag is interpreted as follows:
.TP
.B "bit 15 (0x8000)"
Byte-shift flag. 8086 code is byte-oriented rather than word-aligned,
so the 16-bit value actually being relocated routinely does not begin
at the tag's own nominal 2-byte-aligned offset. If this bit is set,
the relocated word is the one starting one byte
.B past
that nominal offset, rather than the nominal offset itself. (This is a
MUTOS extension; it has no counterpart in stock Unix V7, where every
relocatable value is word-aligned by construction on the PDP-11.)
.TP
.B "bits 1\(en3 (mask 016), relocation type"
.RS
.TP
.B R_ABS
(0x00) No further symbol reference; only the byte-shift interpretation
above (and, when the low bit is set, the segment's own PC-relative
adjustment) applies.
.TP
.B R_TEXT
(0x02) Relocate relative to the final text segment origin.
.TP
.B R_DATA
(0x04) Relocate relative to the final data segment origin.
.TP
.B R_BSS
(0x06) Relocate relative to the final bss segment origin.
.TP
.B R_EXT
(0x08) Relocate relative to an external symbol; see below.
.RE
.TP
.B "bit 0 (0x01)"
PC-relative flag: if set, the segment's own new origin is subtracted
from the relocated value after the addition above, as in stock V7.
.TP
.B "bits 4\(en14 (mask 0x7FF0, shifted right 4), symbol index"
Only present, and only meaningful, when the type field (bits 1\(en3) is
.BR R_EXT :
a zero-based index into the input file's own local symbol-numbering
sequence (the order in which symbol table entries occur in that file),
identifying which symbol the word is relocated against.
.B "This is a MUTOS extension of the V7 relocation-word layout: the
symbol-index field here is only 11 bits wide (bits 4\(en14), not 12
bits (bits 4\(en15) as in stock V7's ld.c"
\(em bit 15 is reserved for the byte-shift flag described above and is
never part of the symbol index, even for an
.B R_EXT
tag.
.SH "ARCHIVE FORMAT"
Archive
.RB ( .a )
libraries use the legacy Unix V7
.IR ar (1)
layout unmodified, including its PDP-11 middle-endian
.B long
fields; this is the one place in the whole MUTOS 1700 toolchain where
middle-endian encoding is required on disk. An archive begins with the
16-bit little-endian magic word
.B MUTOS_ARCMAGIC
(0177545), followed by a sequence of archive members, each consisting
of a fixed 26-byte member header immediately followed by that member's
content (padded with one trailing NUL byte if the content length is
odd, so that the next member header again starts on an even offset).
.SS "Member header (struct archdr, 26 bytes)"
.PP
.nf
.ta 1.4i
typedef struct {
    char     aname[15];  /* 14 archive bytes + guaranteed NUL terminator */
    uint32_t atime;      /* PDP-11 middle-endian */
    uint8_t  auid;
    uint8_t  agid;
    uint16_t amode;      /* plain 16-bit little-endian, like a.out fields */
    uint32_t asize;      /* PDP-11 middle-endian */
} mutos_archdr_t;
.fi
.PP
.B MUTOS_ARCHDR_SIZE
(26) gives the on-disk size of the header (note that the in-memory
.B mutos_archdr_t.aname
is one byte larger, 15 bytes, to guarantee NUL termination after
.IR mutos_archdr_read ()
copies the 14 on-disk bytes).
.B aname
is the member's (file) name, NUL-padded to 14 on-disk bytes.
.B atime
is the member's modification time, a PDP-11 middle-endian 32-bit Unix
timestamp.
.B auid
and
.B agid
are the owning user and group IDs.
.B amode
is the member's file mode, an ordinary 16-bit little-endian value
(unlike
.B atime
and
.BR asize ,
this field is
.B not
a
.B long
in the original and is therefore not middle-endian encoded).
.B asize
is the member content's size in bytes, PDP-11 middle-endian.
.SS "__.SYMDEF table of contents"
An archive processed by
.IR ranlib (1)
has, as its first member, one named
.B __.SYMDEF
(the literal string used for this comparison is available as the
historical constant
.I goodnm
in the original V7
.IR ld.c ,
reproduced verbatim as
.I "__.SYMDEF"
in this project). Its content is a packed array of
.B "struct tab"
entries (see below); the member's own
.B atime
(from its
.B archdr )
records when the table of contents was last regenerated, letting
.IR ld (1)
detect a stale table by comparing it against the archive file's
current on-host modification time. See
.IR mutos_ld (1)
.B ARCHIVES
for how the three resulting cases (no
.BR __.SYMDEF ,
up-to-date, or stale) are handled during linking.
.SS "struct tab (ranlib table-of-contents entry, 12 bytes)"
.PP
.nf
.ta 1.4i
typedef struct {
    char     cname[9];   /* 8 archive bytes + guaranteed NUL terminator */
    uint32_t cloc;       /* PDP-11 middle-endian */
} mutos_tab_t;
.fi
.PP
.B MUTOS_TAB_SIZE
(12) gives the on-disk size of one table-of-contents entry (with the
in-memory
.B mutos_tab_t.cname
again one byte larger than the on-disk field, for NUL termination).
.B cname
is a defined external symbol's name, NUL-padded to 8 on-disk bytes.
.B cloc
is the byte offset, within the archive file, of the
.B archdr
of the archive member that defines
.BR cname .
.SH "SERIALIZATION HELPERS"
All functions below are
.B "static inline"
and operate directly on raw byte buffers, independent of host struct
layout and host byte order.
.TP
.BI "uint16_t mutos_get_u16le(const uint8_t *" p )
.TQ
.BI "void mutos_put_u16le(uint8_t *" p ", uint16_t " v )
Read/write a plain 16-bit little-endian value at
.IR p .
Used for every multi-byte field of the exec header, symbol table, and
the
.B amode
field of an archive header.
.TP
.BI "uint32_t mutos_get_u32_pdp11(const uint8_t *" p )
.TQ
.BI "void mutos_put_u32_pdp11(uint8_t *" p ", uint32_t " v )
Read/write a 32-bit PDP-11 middle-endian value at
.IR p :
the high-order 16-bit word first, each word itself little-endian.
Used only for the
.BR atime ,
.B asize
and
.B cloc
fields of the archive format.
.TP
.BI "void mutos_hdr_read(const uint8_t *" buf ", mutos_hdr_t *" h )
.TQ
.BI "void mutos_hdr_write(uint8_t *" buf ", const mutos_hdr_t *" h )
(De)serialize a 16-byte exec header between
.I buf
and
.IR h .
.TP
.BI "void mutos_sym_read(const uint8_t *" buf ", mutos_sym_t *" s )
.TQ
.BI "void mutos_sym_write(uint8_t *" buf ", const mutos_sym_t *" s )
(De)serialize a 12-byte symbol table entry between
.I buf
and
.IR s .
.TP
.BI "void mutos_archdr_read(const uint8_t *" buf ", mutos_archdr_t *" h )
(Deserialize only; the toolchain does not currently write archives) a
26-byte archive member header from
.I buf
into
.IR h ,
NUL-terminating
.I h\->aname
in the process.
.TP
.BI "void mutos_tab_read(const uint8_t *" buf ", mutos_tab_t *" t )
(Deserialize only) a 12-byte
.B "struct tab"
entry from
.I buf
into
.IR t ,
NUL-terminating
.I t\->cname
in the process.
.SH "CONSTANTS SUMMARY"
.PP
.nf
.ta 2.2i
MUTOS_HDR_SIZE      16   exec header size, bytes
MUTOS_SYM_SIZE       12   symbol table entry size, bytes
MUTOS_ARCHDR_SIZE    26   archive member header size, bytes
MUTOS_TAB_SIZE        12   ranlib table-of-contents entry size, bytes
MUTOS_ARCMAGIC   0177545   archive file magic (first 16-bit word)
A_MAGIC1 (FMAGIC)    0407   normal
A_MAGIC2 (NMAGIC)    0410   read-only shareable text
A_MAGIC3 (IMAGIC)    0411   separate I&D
A_MAGIC4 (OMAGIC)    0405   overlay / plain
.fi
.SH NOTES
None of the on-disk formats described here are portable to the host's
native struct layout by direct overlay: the exec header and symbol
table pack tightly with no padding, and the archive format mixes
16-bit little-endian and 32-bit PDP-11-middle-endian fields within the
same 26-byte header. Always go through the
.B mutos_*_read()
/
.B mutos_*_write()
helpers above rather than casting a buffer to one of the struct types
directly; on a 64-bit host, a direct cast would additionally be
subject to the host compiler's own alignment padding, which does not
match the on-disk layout at all.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR mutos_ld (1)
.SH HISTORY
This header was derived from the original Unix Version 7
.I a_out.h
(referenced in this project as
.IR v7_a_out.h ),
adapted for the MUTOS 1700 target on the Robotron A7100/A7150. Several
details that are not present in, or that deviate from, stock V7 were
determined by direct comparison against object, executable, and
archive files produced by real MUTOS 1700 hardware and toolchain
output rather than from any surviving written specification: the
unconditional 8-byte (and conditional 64-byte) text-segment padding
performed by
.IR ld (1),
the pure segment-local (rather than V7's preceding-segment-size-baked-in)
interpretation of data/bss-relative raw symbol and content values, the
byte-shift flag and 11-bit (rather than 12-bit) symbol-index field of
the relocation word format, and the archive format's continued use of
PDP-11 middle-endian
.B long
fields even though the a.out header itself was redefined as pure
16-bit little-endian.
.SH BUGS
Archive
.B write
support (as opposed to read/parse support) is not implemented by this
header's helper functions, since the toolchain does not currently
produce archives itself.
